WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.044 --> 00:00:17.717
The Mediterranean Sea is bordered by orange trees and rugged mountains.

2
00:00:17.718 --> 00:00:21.884
The air is pure and perfumed with the scent of myrtle.

3
00:00:21.885 --> 00:00:27.360
But Captain Nemo was in a hurry, so I experienced none of the Mediterranean's enchantments.

4
00:00:27.361 --> 00:00:36.344
Starting from the shores of Greece on the morning of February 16, we traveled at full speed and reached the Atlantic Ocean 48 hours later.

5
00:00:36.345 --> 00:00:41.685
It was obvious to me that Captain Nemo wanted to avoid the countries surrounding the Mediterranean.

6
00:00:41.686 --> 00:00:47.090
The breeze probably brought back too many memories, and perhaps too many regrets.

7
00:00:47.091 --> 00:00:51.773
We were traveling so quickly that Ned had to give up any hope of escape.

8
00:00:51.774 --> 00:00:57.677
It would have been impossible to launch the small boat when we were traveling thirty-five to forty feet per second.

9
00:00:57.678 --> 00:01:01.018
He might as well have jumped off a train going at full speed.

10
00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:08.669
I followed our route on the gallery map and calculated that we had traveled ten thousand leagues in three and a half months.

11
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:15.987
Later in the morning of February 18, the Nautilus rose to the surface, so we could take our daily walk upon the platform.

12
00:01:15.988 --> 00:01:19.820
I went up at once, accompanied by Ned and Conseil.

13
00:01:19.821 --> 00:01:26.058
But the Atlantic Ocean was so rough that the submarine rocked violently with every wave that crashed over it.

14
00:01:26.059 --> 00:01:32.169
We soon decided to go below. Conseil returned to his cabin while Ned followed me to mine.

15
00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:36.225
When my door was shut, he sat down and looked at me silently.

16
00:01:36.226 --> 00:01:43.145
"Ned, my friend," I said, "I know how disappointed you must be that we couldn't escape while in the Mediterranean."

17
00:01:43.146 --> 00:01:50.455
The harpooner did not respond, yet the crease in his brow and the expression on his face showed me that he was thinking hard.

18
00:01:50.456 --> 00:01:56.550
"I don't think we should give up yet," I continued. "We're going up the coast of Portugal.

19
00:01:56.551 --> 00:02:02.288
France and England are not far away, and we can easily find refuge in one of those countries.

20
00:02:02.289 --> 00:02:08.963
Now if the Nautilus were traveling south, I'd share your uneasiness, but I think you'll be able to act in a few days."

21
00:02:08.964 --> 00:02:14.717
Ned stared at me for a few more moments before he spoke. "We're leaving tonight."

22
00:02:14.718 --> 00:02:18.510
I was so startled by this statement that words failed me.

23
00:02:18.511 --> 00:02:23.941
"We agreed to wait for an opportunity," Ned went on, "and that opportunity has arrived.

24
00:02:23.942 --> 00:02:27.193
Tonight we'll be only a few miles from the Spanish coast.

25
00:02:27.194 --> 00:02:31.018
It's cloudy and windy—good conditions for an escape."

26
00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:35.002
As I was still silent, Ned stood up and faced me.

27
00:02:35.003 --> 00:02:40.907
"We leave tonight at nine o'clock," he said. "I've already told Conseil to be ready.

28
00:02:40.908 --> 00:02:45.089
At that time Captain Nemo will be in his room, probably asleep.

29
00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:51.168
Conseil and I will go up the central staircase, and you'll remain nearby in the library, awaiting my signal.

30
00:02:51.169 --> 00:02:59.073
The oars, mast, and sail are already in the small boat, and I've even gotten us some provisions. So everything is ready."

31
00:02:59.074 --> 00:03:04.145
"But the sea is so rough," I said, finally finding my voice.

32
00:03:04.146 --> 00:03:09.311
"I agree, but we have a sturdy boat and we'll be traveling only a few miles.

33
00:03:09.312 --> 00:03:12.216
Who knows where the Nautilus will be tomorrow?

34
00:03:12.217 --> 00:03:19.874
We could be hundreds of leagues away, so we have to take this chance tonight. I will see you later, at nine o'clock."

35
00:03:19.875 --> 00:03:24.311
With these words Ned departed, leaving me feeling numb.

36
00:03:24.312 --> 00:03:30.700
I had always imagined that when the time came to escape, there would be an opportunity to discuss it.

37
00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:35.216
But Ned had given me no time to think, never mind talk, over the matter.

38
00:03:35.217 --> 00:03:39.122
I knew he was right; this was a good opportunity to escape.

39
00:03:39.123 --> 00:03:42.367
But it was still hard to believe that the moment had arrived.

40
00:03:42.368 --> 00:03:47.097
At that point I heard the sounds that meant the submarine was sinking under the waves.

41
00:03:47.098 --> 00:03:54.017
I passed the rest of the day torn between the thrill of freedom and the sadness of abandoning my underwater studies.

42
00:03:54.018 --> 00:04:02.096
Sometimes I hoped our escape would succeed, and other times I wished something would happen to prevent us from leaving the submarine.

43
00:04:02.097 --> 00:04:07.096
Twice I went to the gallery to check if we had changed our course. But no.

44
00:04:07.097 --> 00:04:13.525
The Nautilus was still cruising up the coast of Portugal. This day of waiting seemed to last forever.

45
00:04:13.526 --> 00:04:17.779
My supper was served in my room as usual, but I could eat little.

46
00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:26.914
At seven o'clock, there were still 120 minutes left. I couldn't sit quietly anymore so I went to see the gallery for the last time.

47
00:04:26.915 --> 00:04:34.406
I spent a long while admiring its treasures, and I wished the panels were open so I could gaze underwater one more time.

48
00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:43.065
A clock struck eight, startling me. I returned to my room and dressed warmly. I wasn't taking anything with me besides my notes.

49
00:04:43.066 --> 00:04:50.223
At a few minutes before nine, I pressed my ear to the door that linked my cabin and the captain's. Silence.

50
00:04:50.224 --> 00:04:53.398
I returned to the gallery and then went to the library.

51
00:04:53.399 --> 00:04:57.977
I stood at the door leading to the central staircase and awaited Ned's signal.

52
00:04:57.978 --> 00:05:05.731
Suddenly I heard the submarine's propeller slow down and stop. The only sound came from my heart beating loudly.

53
00:05:05.732 --> 00:05:11.128
I felt a slight shock and knew that the Nautilus had stopped on the sea bottom.

54
00:05:11.129 --> 00:05:14.890
Something was not right and there was no signal from Ned.

55
00:05:14.891 --> 00:05:19.350
"There you are!" Captain Nemo entered the library from the gallery.

56
00:05:19.351 --> 00:05:26.199
He was smiling broadly. "I've been looking all over for you, Professor. Do you know the history of Spain?"

57
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:31.294
"What?" I was still startled by the captain's arrival in the midst of my escape.

58
00:05:31.295 --> 00:05:34.104
"I asked if you knew the history of Spain."

59
00:05:34.105 --> 00:05:42.294
Captain Nemo went on to describe a famous sea battle where Spanish ships, loaded with silver and gold, were sunk in 1702.

60
00:05:42.295 --> 00:05:49.254
"We're right at the shipwreck site. If you come with me, you'll likely be very interested in what I have to show you."

61
00:05:49.255 --> 00:05:55.591
By this point, I'd recovered my composure, so I followed him into the gallery, where the panels were now open.

62
00:05:55.592 --> 00:05:58.437
I looked out and saw a shipwreck.

63
00:05:58.438 --> 00:06:05.659
Members of the submarine's crew were carrying silver and gold bars as well as precious jewels back to the Nautilus.

64
00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:14.159
"This treasure, as well as other shipwrecks that I've marked on a map, are the source of my great wealth," Captain Nemo explained.

65
00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.746
"But doesn't this all belong to someone?" I asked.

66
00:06:17.747 --> 00:06:23.643
"The Spanish government? And even if it doesn't, aren't there poor people who could benefit from all these riches?"

67
00:06:23.644 --> 00:06:27.564
As soon as I spoke, I saw that I had wounded the captain.

68
00:06:27.565 --> 00:06:31.897
"Do you think I collect these treasures for myself?" asked Captain Nemo.

69
00:06:31.898 --> 00:06:40.643
"Do you think I'm ignorant of all the suffering people on this earth, miserable creatures who should be consoled, victims who should be avenged?"

70
00:06:40.644 --> 00:06:46.389
And then I understood for whom those gold bars in the Grecian islands were destined.

71
00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:55.528
My ruined escape plans seemed like such a small problem compared to the misery of those people who needed the captain's philanthropy.
